From ff7a26a69e6bed64047aa13ac9db35a9a9d63f9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 16:38:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10437/11362] Generalization: union across batches within one
 policy, never freshest-wins

Two bugs in my own dashboard, both found by reading its output rather than by a
test.

FRESHEST-WINS ERASED A REAL MEASUREMENT. The eval must run in small batches -- it
writes only at the end, so one long invocation risks hours producing nothing --
and each batch covers DIFFERENT games. Picking the newest file therefore picks
one batch, not the measurement. It regressed the moment a misfired batch wrote
`per_game: []` and, being newer, replaced "2 levels [policy=e3]" with
"not measured". A newer file is not a better one. Now a union keyed by game, so
an empty batch contributes nothing and cannot erase other batches, and a
re-measure updates only its own game.

THEN THE UNION POOLED INCOMPARABLE POLICIES. The first union read
"policy=e3,explorer" -- summing the no-LLM tier-1 floor with the full cascade,
inventing a number neither run measured. Same discipline as refusing to pool the
native and selfparse transports: e3 is preferred when present and other policies
are excluded because they are a different agent, not because they are old.

Three earlier tests of mine asserted the old behaviour, including one that
asserted freshest-wins directly. Updated to the corrected semantics with the
reason recorded in each, rather than deleted -- a test that encoded a wrong rule
is evidence about the rule, and the correction belongs beside it.

26 tests; 3/3 mutations RED (empty batch erasing the union, policies pooled
again, only the last file counting). Restores byte-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw
